		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4400&amp;p=34381#p34381"><![CDATA[
<blockquote class="uncited"><div>Thank you for the explanations, very helpful -<br><br>I already stumbled across this line when I was inspecting the display code <div class="codebox"><p>CODE: </p><pre><code>*((volatile uint8_t*) LCD_FSMC_ADDR_DATA) = 0;// write 0 to the display pins , to pull them all low, so keyboard reads don't need to</code></pre></div>And I fully agree that setting the pins to output again - do nothing - and resetting them back to input should not really have an effect. <br><br>But I tested my device now for several hours and used the keypad and buttons intensively. It works perfectly.<br><br>I zipped and attached the files below. <br>Maybe you can manage to create a build and test with the device of your friend you mentioned in another post above. <br>It would be interesting to see if it also works for his device. <br><br><br>Recarding the CPU .<br><br>CPU : "STM"<br>CPU Sig: 0x0413<br>CPU Rev: 0x1007<br>CPU Pack: 0x0005</div></blockquote>OK<br><br>Looks like when the pin functionality is changed from FSMC to GPIO, that there is probably a glitch , which may randomly drive the pins high for a short time before.<br><br>Hence your code of setting them to Output <span style="text-decoration:underline">probably</span> drives them low.<br><br>However, I think for safety, that the GPIO pins should be set to Low i.e "GPIO_PIN_RESET". <br><br>by adding these 2 lines to your code, if you didn't already do that<br><div class="codebox"><p>CODE: </p><pre><code>HAL_GPIO_WritePin((GPIOD, LCD_D0_Pin | LCD_D1_Pin | LCD_D2_Pin | LCD_D3_Pin, GPIO_PIN_RESET);HAL_GPIO_WritePin((GPIOE, LCD_D4_Pin | LCD_D5_Pin | LCD_D6_Pin | LCD_D7_Pin, GPIO_PIN_RESET);</code></pre></div>Also, the time the pins are being driven low will be quite short, and I would also be tempted to add a small for(;;) loop delay between driving the pins low and setting then to input<br><br><br>Setting the pins low is probably the most important and the delay would be optional.<br><br>Could you please try adding my code, and confirm there are no adverse effects.<br><br>Thanks<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=50">VK3KYY</a> — Thu Dec 05, 2024 7:18 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
