		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Re: RT3S GPS - keyboard stops responding built 20240908143155]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4400&amp;p=34376#p34376"><![CDATA[
Without seeing all the code, its difficult to know exactly what you did<br><br>However it looks like you added another block of code like this<br><br><br><div class="codebox"><p>CODE: </p><pre><code>GPIO_InitStruct.Mode = GPIO_MODE_OUTPUT;GPIO_InitStruct.Speed = GPIO_SPEED_FREQ_LOW;GPIO_InitStruct.Pull = GPIO_PULLDOWN;GPIO_InitStruct.Pin = LCD_D0_Pin | LCD_D1_Pin | LCD_D2_Pin | LCD_D3_Pin;HAL_GPIO_Init(GPIOD, &amp;GPIO_InitStruct);GPIO_InitStruct.Pin = LCD_D4_Pin | LCD_D5_Pin | LCD_D6_Pin | LCD_D7_Pin;HAL_GPIO_Init(GPIOE, &amp;GPIO_InitStruct);</code></pre></div>Which sets the 'input' pins to output, and effectly kept the previous code that sets the pins to input.<br><br>This probably has the effect of driving the pins low, but you should probably also have also added<br><br><div class="codebox"><p>CODE: </p><pre><code>HAL_GPIO_WritePin((GPIOD, LCD_D0_Pin | LCD_D1_Pin | LCD_D2_Pin | LCD_D3_Pin, GPIO_PIN_RESET);HAL_GPIO_WritePin((GPIOE, LCD_D4_Pin | LCD_D5_Pin | LCD_D6_Pin | LCD_D7_Pin, GPIO_PIN_RESET);</code></pre></div><br>before the call to HAL_GPIO_Init()<br><br>If ypu look in main.c (which is automatically generated code from the STM32 Cube IDE), you will see that it had multiple calls to HAL_GPIO_WritePin() before calling Init<br><br>e.g.<br><br><br><div class="codebox"><p>CODE: </p><pre><code>  HAL_GPIO_WritePin(GPIOE, DMR_SPI_CS_Pin|C6000_PWD_Pin, GPIO_PIN_SET);   </code></pre></div><br><br>The keyboard input pins are shared with the display output pins, and the same pins are written to by the FSMC controller part of the MCU during display updates<br><br>I checked the code and we do set the FSMC output register to 0  in the renderRows() function<br><div class="codebox"><p>CODE: </p><pre><code>*((volatile uint8_t*) LCD_FSMC_ADDR_DATA) = 0;// write 0 to the display pins , to pull them all low, so keyboard reads don't need to</code></pre></div><br>So this should have left the pins driven low, and it should not have been necessary to set the GPIO pins to Output before setting them to input<br><br><br>However, its possible that the MCU has some "Errata" aka bugs in its silicon.<br><br><br>Can you look in the Firmware Info screen, and check whether your radio has a genuine STM MCU or whether it has a Chinese clone.<br><br>If the CPU type is "STM" its genuine,  if it reads "TYT" then its the unknown Chinese clone , fitted by TYT and probably also the same clone used in the Baofeng radio.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=50">VK3KYY</a> — Wed Dec 04, 2024 11:20 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
	</entry>
