		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Re: RT3S GPS - keyboard stops responding built 20240908143155]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4400&amp;p=34349#p34349"><![CDATA[
<blockquote class="uncited"><div>Ok I tried to modify the <br><br>void displayWriteCmd(uint8_t cmd)<br>{<br>    *((volatile uint8_t*) LCD_FSMC_ADDR_COMMAND) = cmd;<br>}<br><br>That it just returns.. (removed the line to write the cmd)<br><br><br>The display stays white of of course. But I was in VFO mode and entered a "1" short after that i was able to hear beep sounds. They are coming from the "'0" entered until a 100.0000 frequency is there and then for every additional "0'" a beep is hearable indicating a not valid frequency. <br><br>That proofs that removing the line in desplayWriteCmd does not help. <br><br>Any other ideas I can try?  <br><br>And yes to increase the settling time does not remove the problem. I even tried with factor 10...</div></blockquote>I don't know if this makes much difference in Push Pull mode, but you could try changing<br><br><div class="codebox"><p>CODE: </p><pre><code>GPIO_InitStruct.Pull = GPIO_NOPULL;</code></pre></div>to<br><div class="codebox"><p>CODE: </p><pre><code>GPIO_InitStruct.Pull = GPIO_PULLDOWN;</code></pre></div><br>Also.<br><br>I don't know if the GPIO port speed actually needs to be set to Low<br><br><div class="codebox"><p>CODE: </p><pre><code>GPIO_InitStruct.Speed = GPIO_SPEED_FREQ_LOW;</code></pre></div>you could try one of the other values e.g.<br><div class="codebox"><p>CODE: </p><pre><code>#define  GPIO_SPEED_FREQ_LOW         0x00000000U  /*!&lt; IO works at 2 MHz, please refer to the product datasheet */#define  GPIO_SPEED_FREQ_MEDIUM      0x00000001U  /*!&lt; range 12,5 MHz to 50 MHz, please refer to the product datasheet */#define  GPIO_SPEED_FREQ_HIGH        0x00000002U  /*!&lt; range 25 MHz to 100 MHz, please refer to the product datasheet  */#define  GPIO_SPEED_FREQ_VERY_HIGH   0x00000003U  /*!&lt; range 50 MHz to 200 MHz, please refer to the product datasheet  */</code></pre></div><br>Also a few other thoughts<br><br>Is your backlight set to 100% and constantly on ?   Otherwise perhaps you have some form of signal injection caused by the backlight control signal or the supply voltage fluction to the display board becasue of the backlight.<br><br>i.e the front PC is connected via a thin ribbon cable and there have been problems on other types of radio becasue the GND connection via the ribbon is not ideal.<br><br>Also. if the problem is some sort of noise pickup, you could attempt to implement something similar to bebounce functionality,  e.g.<br><br>in<br><div class="codebox"><p>CODE: </p><pre><code>for (size_t k = 0; k &lt; KEYBOARD_KEYS_PER_ROW; k++){if((KeyboardMatrix[i].Rows[k].Key != KEY_NONE) &amp;&amp;(HAL_GPIO_ReadPin(KeyboardMatrix[i].Rows[k].GPIOPort, KeyboardMatrix[i].Rows[k].GPIOPin) == GPIO_PIN_SET)){result = KeyboardMatrix[i].Rows[k].Key;break;}}</code></pre></div>modify the code so that it checks each input pin multiple times, with a small delay between each check and only if the pin was High for all iterations, should it be accepted as a valid keypress<br><br><br><br>There are probably also other things you could try.  Are you a professional programmer??<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=50">VK3KYY</a> — Wed Dec 04, 2024 2:44 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
